 "name": "Snow",
 "_note": "The brief's one explicit instruction about snow is that it must not look like vertical white lines, and four decisions here are aimed at exactly that. (1) A flake is a CARD, not a dot: shape2d 'leaf' with a low tumbleRate and an aspect near 1 gives a slab that turns edge-on twice a revolution, so the field twinkles as it falls because the flakes are presenting different areas, not because anything is flickering. (2) velocityStretch is ZERO on every layer. A smeared flake reads as a spark and a column of smeared flakes reads as vertical white lines, which is the failure by name. (3) dragSizeBias 0.95 plus sizeSkew 2.8: a small dense flake falls fast and a large one hangs, so at any instant the field has a spread of speeds rather than a single rate, which is what makes it read as falling rather than as scrolling. (4) Turbulence at a LOW spatial frequency (turbulenceScale 0.18) with windInfluence: the flakes wander together in slow eddies rather than each jittering on its own, because neighbouring flakes in the world are in the same parcel of air. 'snow-near' is fifty very large, very soft, nearly transparent flakes right at the lens -- the out-of-focus foreground the brief asks for, and the layer that sells the depth. Render a short range and take the last frame: the first frame of a render has deltaTime 0, so the tumble is right but nothing is motion blurred.",
